.\" Source: src/cwd.c, src/morse.c
.TH "CWD" "1" "October 23, 2026" "ka9q-radio" "ka9q-radio Manual"
.SH NAME
cwd \- generate Morse-code audio from text written to a FIFO
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cwd
.RI "[-v] [-I fifo] [-s ssrc] [-S wpm] [-P Hz] [-L dB] -R destination"
.SH STATUS
.B Not actively maintained
.SH DESCRIPTION
cwd creates or opens a named pipe, reads text lines, converts them to Morse
audio, and sends paced RTP packets. Output is mono signed 16-bit big-endian
PCM at 8000 samples/s. It is useful for announcements and repeater
identification. The program runs in the foreground and retains a writer on the
FIFO so closing a client writer does not terminate it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B "\-I pathname"
Set input FIFO, default /run/cw/input. The parent directory must exist and be
writable. A missing FIFO is created with mode 0666.
.TP
.B "\-R destination"
Set RTP destination, required. Multicast TTL is fixed at one.
.TP
.B "\-s ssrc"
Set output SSRC. Default 100.
.TP
.B "\-S wpm"
Set Morse speed in words per minute. Default 18.
.TP
.B "\-P Hz"
Set audio pitch in Hz. Default 500.
.TP
.B "\-L dB"
Set tone amplitude level in dB relative to unit peak amplitude. Default -29.
The encoder uses the negative absolute value of this argument.
.TP
.B "\-v"
Increase diagnostics.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The process locale determines supported input characters. Select a suitable
UTF-8 locale for accented letters and other supported Morse alphabets.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run the generator, then write a message from another shell:
.PP
.nf
cwd \-R 239.1.2.4:5004 \-I /tmp/cw\-input
printf 'TEST DE KA9Q\en' > /tmp/cw\-input
.fi
.SH LIMITATIONS
The destination domain name must be resolvable; cwd does not advertise it.
No channel-status metadata is generated. A receiver must recognize the RTP
payload format. There is no -V or --help option in the current parser.
.SH SEE ALSO
monitor(1), radiod(8), mkfifo(1)
.PP
Project documentation: https://github.com/ka9q/ka9q-radio
